1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And let me just say again, then the
subspace that has only that one point,

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
that point is zero
dimension, of course.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And the basis is empty.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
If the dimension is zero,
there shouldn't be anybody in the basis.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:26.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So the basis of that
smallest subspace is the empty set.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And the number of members
in the empty set is zero, so that's the dimension.

7
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Okay.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Good.

9
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:43.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
Now, I have just five
minutes to tell you about, well, actually about some, some,

10
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:55.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
this is now this last topic
of small world graphs, and leads into a lecture about graphs.

11
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
And maybe a relative of
that.

12
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:06.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
But let me tell you the, in
these last minutes, the graphs that I am interested in.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
It's the graph where, so
what is a graph?

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
Better tell you that first.

15
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Okay.

16
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
What's a graph?

17
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Okay.

18
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
This isn't calculus.

19
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
We're not, I'm not thinking
of like some sine curve.

20
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
The word graph is used in a
completely different way.

21
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:37.000 line:65% position:93% align:right  region:middle_right
It's a set of, a bunch of nodes
and edges.

22
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.000 line:65% position:93% align:right  region:middle_right
Ages connecting the nodes.

23
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.000 line:65% position:93% align:right  region:middle_right
So I have nodes like five nodes.

24
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
And edges, I'll put in some
edges.

25
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
I could, could include them
all.

26
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
Well, let me put in a
couple more.

27
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:04.000 line:90% position:7%  align:left   region:bottom_left
There's a graph with five
nodes and one, two, three, four, five, six edges.

28
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:11.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And some five by six matrix
is going to tell us everything about that graph.

29
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Let me leave that matrix
the next time and tell you about the question I'm interested in.

30
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
Suppose, suppose the graph,
it just doesn't have this five nodes,

31
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:33.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
but suppose every, suppose
every person in this room is a node.

32
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:41.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And suppose there's an edge
between two nodes if those two people are friends.

33
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So have I described the
graph?

34
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
It's a pretty big graph,
100 nodes.

35
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And I don't know how many
edges they're in there.

36
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
There's an edge if your
friends.

37
00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So that's the graph for
this class.

38
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
A similar graph you could
take for the whole country.

39
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:13.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
So 260 million nodes and
edges between friends.

40
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:26.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
And the question for that
graph is how many steps does it take to get from anybody to anybody?

41
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:33.000 line:90% position:93% align:right  region:bottom_right
What two people are
furthest apart?
